WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.968
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.002
We're here with Jesse Eisenberg.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.703
You and your family
live in New York.

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.205
Are you spending
the holidays here?

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:07.373
-No. No, we're not.

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.042
We're spending the holidays
in the Midwest

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:12.378
at an international
folk dancing camp.

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:13.746
-Okay.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:16.516
You could tell I-I obviously
lost the, you know, battle

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:17.717
on making that decision.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.186
-Yeah.
-Have you done it before?

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.921
-We did it once.
We did it this summer.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:25.625
It's folk dancing, you know,
from mainly the Balkans.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.959
So, you know what that is,
of course.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.261
-Yeah, of course. Yeah, yeah.
-Of course. It's classic.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.495
-I didn't have
to go to school for it.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:30.629
I just knew it.
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:32.799
You either have it inside
or not.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.501
It's dancing.
No, but, like --

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:36.936
You know, so you live
in these cabins

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.538
and then, you know,
in the afternoons

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.607
you go and do these, like,
long line dancing exercises,

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:42.941
I guess you might call it.

00:00:42.941 --> 00:00:45.478
And I didn't go out
for, like, the first three days

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.514
because I heard it was,
you know, dancing.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:50.049
And then, I finally went out

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.818
most likely to, like,
retrieve my wife

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.853
to, like, bring her back
to, you know, reality.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:57.056
And then, I discovered that
it's not so much dancing

00:00:57.056 --> 00:01:00.326
as kind of, like, formations
and geometry and rules.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.695
And those are three things
I love.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.263
-[ Laughs ]
-And, in fact,

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.565
I think I love them as much
as I hate dancing, you know.

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:08.401
-Oh, wow.
-So then I started, like,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.568
doing these line dances.

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:10.703
I found myself.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.072
Because it's something physical,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:15.374
but it requires
complete rule-following.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:16.642
-Yeah.
-And to me, that's great.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:17.877
-Gotcha.
-You know what I mean? Yeah.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.712
-So no instructor
is ever, like,

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.414
"Find yourself!"?
-No. No.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.283
-Okay.
-That's my worst nightmare.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:25.351
That's what I expect

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.820
everything that's
dancing-related to be.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:28.954
I remember, actually,
when my wife and I

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:30.156
first started dating,
she would go out

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:32.492
to dance clubs
and it was just completely --

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:33.759
I was never even asked.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:36.128
-It was just understood,
"I'm going out dancing.

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:37.196
I'll probably see you
in the morning."

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.398
-[ Laughs ]
-And that was great.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:40.666
-So that's wonderful
that you found

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:42.001
this collective thing
to do together,

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.636
which is to go
to Balkan folk dancing class.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.505
-Balkan -- A perfect
compromise, yeah.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.874
-You grew up in New Jersey?

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.475
-Yes.
-What was when you --
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.311
There we go.
We have some in the house.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:52.478
-I would hope so.
-Always sneak through,

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.214
no matter how much
security's at the door.

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:56.449
[ Laughter ]
-Well, probably because
of the proximity.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:57.650
-Yeah, the proximity.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.586
-What did you think was funny
growing up?

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:01.621
What were your...

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.656
-You know, I wrote jo--

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.124
I mean, it's weird to tell you
this because I mean, you know --

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:08.561
But I wrote, like, just jokes
when I was younger.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.496
You know, I saw, recently,
in my parents' house,

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:12.798
in a drawer, I just had written,
like, tons and tons

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.933
of one-liners
when I was younger,

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:16.869
kind of Borscht Belt-y
one-liners, and they --

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:18.471
I was going through them
kind of horrified

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.974
'cause they were, like, really
all about one of two things.

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:23.142
They were either
about basketball or sex

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.310
'cause those were, like,
the two things

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.411
I was interested in.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.013
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:29.115
-Maybe because I knew
those were the two things

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.384
that I was probably not going to
achieve in my life, you know.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.652
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.553
And one I achieved, you know,

00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:36.689
a few times over the course
of a given year.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.457
-Right. [ Laughs ]
-And the other one

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.626
I can't seem to get
a foothold in,

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.595
you know what I mean?

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.963
But anyway, those were,
like, my jokes

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.899
because I was just
obsessed with basketball

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:48.401
and I heard jokes about sex.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.869
I couldn't even tell you
probably what it was.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.270
I was 13 years old, you know.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.572
I probably didn't even know
what I was talking about.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:56.042
But that was, like --
Jokes were about sex.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:57.610
I guess jokes are still
kind of about sex.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.512
-They are still.
And then, did you also write --

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:03.482
You didn't write a Woody Allen-
inspired screenplay.

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:05.184
-I did, and then I wrote
a screenplay, yes.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:06.686
When I was 16,
I wrote a screenplay

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.054
about Woody Allen.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.988
-So you wrote it about him?

00:03:09.988 --> 00:03:13.226
-I wrote it about Woody Allen.
Took place modern day.

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:15.695
But it was about him
changing his name to Woody,

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:18.230
which he actually did at the age
at which I wrote it,

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.666
which was, like, 16 years old.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:22.301
And I wrote the script, and I
had sent it around to people

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:24.703
just desperately sending it out
to anybody I can find

00:03:24.703 --> 00:03:25.705
whose address I found.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.740
Anyway, it got to his lawyer

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.042
[ Laughter ]
and, um,

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.844
if you write something
about somebody,

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.046
the person you want to read it

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:34.881
is usually not, you know,
the representative,

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:37.216
you know, the legal counsel
of the person in question.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:39.485
And I remember my dad came
in my bedroom one day,

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:40.853
he was like,
"I have amazing news.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.388
We heard from Woody Allen.
We heard from Woody Allen."

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.722
He had opened the letter.
And I was like,

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.791
"That's incredible."
He was like, "Well, actually,

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:48.161
it's not all great news.
It's not all great news.

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:52.098
The great news ends that
we heard from Woody Allen."

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.432
He sent me
a cease and desist letter.

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:55.568
-[ Laughs ]
-Yeah.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.737
-I gotta say, there's a lot
of 16-year-olds out there

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.739
who aren't getting
cease and desist letters

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.807
from filmmakers.
-No. It's an honor just to be

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:03.041
rejected by him,
you know what I mean?

00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:05.077
-Yeah, exactly.
Hey, thank you for being here.

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.111
Congrats on the show.

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:07.313
It's always a pleasure
to see you.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:08.380
-Thank you so much.
-Have a wonderful time

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.449
at dance camp.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.284
Jesse Eisenberg, everybody.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.020
New episodes of FX's
"Fleishman Is in Trouble"

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.222
stream Thursdays on Hulu.
We'll be right back.

